// Conquord subject: src/fiat2/eval/Kafka_NamedCache.v
//
// THIS IS THE SHARPEST OF THE EIGHT, because this subject's whole difficulty
// is in a write: `sizeInBytes` is a sum over the collection, and what the
// optimizer derives is a maintained total corrected BY A PROBE on the
// delete rather than recomputed.  Cozy's DEFAULT cost model (`--cost-model
// 2`, cost_model.py:21) ranks query asymptotics, then storage size, then
// exact query runtime -- no maintenance term at all; `--cost-model 3` adds
// one `maintenance_cost` per op (:209-212), but UNDER asymptotic query
// runtime and unprioritized among the ops.  So this subject must be run at
// BOTH settings, and the earlier claim that Cozy simply ignores maintenance
// cost was wrong: it ignores it by default and can be asked to weigh it.
//
// Conquord's own two configurations differ only in the order the two choices
// are listed, and the wrong order costs 752x on the delete and takes the
// build from linear to quadratic.  So the question to ask of the output is
// not "is there a map" but "what does delete do to the total".
//
// Encoding decisions:
//   * delete is BY KEY in the source (`cache.remove(key)` at :273), and
//     Cozy's remove takes an element, so it is written as removing
//     `the [...]` at that key -- which is the same operation and, as it
//     happens, the same shape the subject's rewritten delete has.
//   * `put` in the source evicts then inserts (:174-192).  Kept as the two
//     statements it is.
NamedCache:

    type Node = { k : String, size : Int }

    state cache : Bag<Node>

    invariant unique [n.k | n <- cache];

    query get(key : String)
        the [n | n <- cache, n.k == key]

    query sizeInBytes()
        sum [n.size | n <- cache]

    op delete(key : String)
        cache.remove(the [n | n <- cache, n.k == key]);

    op put(node : Node)
        cache.remove(the [n | n <- cache, n.k == node.k]);
        cache.add(node);
